From 03c928dadfc5e07e009ea1d629da260a99b268ad Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: logdog2325 <loganjayfisher@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 18:24:12 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Add a Check for Updates button

Asks GitHub for the newest release, compares it against this build, and offers to
open the download page. Nothing is downloaded or installed automatically.

The build did not previously know which release it was: CI stamps binaries with
upstream Dolphin's git describe, and the only version-looking strings in the fork
are matchmaking compatibility keys that the session index filters on for exact
equality, so they cannot double as a display version. Hence XDNetplay::VERSION,
which must be bumped in the same patch a release is tagged from.

Tags are compared component-wise as integers, because 0.4.9 is older than 0.4.44
and a string comparison says the opposite. A tag that cannot be parsed that way
reports the two versions and lets the user judge, and no failure -- transport
error, rate limit, unparseable reply -- can report "up to date", which would
otherwise hide every future release behind one bad response.

Release title and date are treated as optional, since GitHub returns a null name
for an untitled release; only the tag and the URL decide anything. Both are
remote text: the message box escapes them, or an <a href> in a release title
would render as a live link that opens an arbitrary URL on one click, on the
up-to-date path where no download button is offered at all. The URL is honored
only if it points inside this repository, since both front ends hand it to an OS
opener that acts on more schemes than the web; anything else falls back to the
releases page.
